# Sakura, Sakura (repertoire plan: sakura). The traditional Japanese melody as it is usually
# transcribed, starting on A and ending on E, an octave below the koto, in first position.
# It uses the in scale on E: E F A B C (degrees 1 b2 4 5 b6), the same five notes as A hirajoshi.
# E = string 4 fret 2, F = string 4 fret 3, A = string 3 fret 2, B = open string 2, C = string 2
# fret 1; where the tune dips below E: B = string 5 fret 2, C = string 5 fret 3.
# The words of the traditional verse are written above the phrase they belong to; play it as an
# instrumental, or sing them. A little vibrato (~) on the long fretted notes. No Western chords:
# the teacher's track is an open-string drone, E (string 6) and B (string 2).
# The key line reads E minor because the content checks only read major and minor keys; the tune
# is in the E in scale, with F natural and no G.
title: Sakura, Sakura
id: sakura
composer: Traditional Japanese
source: Edo-period melody printed in 1888 (Tokyo Academy of Music, koto collection); arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (traditional); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 3
tags: melody, japanese, in scale, vibrato, slow
tempo: 66
time: 4/4
key: Em

track: Melody
role: lead
[Sakura]
| ^"Sakura, sakura" q 3:2{2} 3:2 h 2:0 | q 3:2 3:2 h 2:0 |
| ^"yayoi no sora wa" q 3:2 2:0 2:1{1} 2:0 | q 3:2 e 2:0 3:2 h 4:3~{3} |
| ^"miwatasu kagiri" q 4:2{2} 5:3{3} 4:2 4:3{3} | q 4:2 e 4:2 5:3{3} h 5:2~{2} |
| ^"kasumi ka kumo ka" q 3:2 2:0 2:1 2:0 | q 3:2 e 2:0 3:2 h 4:3~ |
| ^"nioi zo izuru" q 4:2 5:3 4:2 4:3 | q 4:2 e 4:2 5:3 h 5:2~ |
| ^"iza ya, iza ya" q 3:2 3:2 h 2:0 | q 3:2 3:2 h 2:0 |
| ^"mi ni yukan" q 4:2 4:3 e 2:0 3:2 q 4:3 | w 4:2~ ||

